# Equating values of a functional property
#
# Alice's birthplace is recorded twice, as :Ghent and as :Gent, and a
# person has only one birthplace (the property is owl:FunctionalProperty).
# An N3 rule concludes that the two names denote the same place
# (owl:sameAs). A second rule is an inference fuse (=> false): it stops the
# run if the data also said the two places were different.

@prefix : <http://example.org/> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix list: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/list#> .
@prefix log: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/log#> .

# A functional property, and two values for it
:birthplace a owl:FunctionalProperty .
:alice :birthplace :Ghent, :Gent .

# Rules for owl:FunctionalProperty
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .

# Two values of a functional property for one subject are the same thing.
# X and Y may also match the same value, so each value is the same as itself.
{
    ?P a owl:FunctionalProperty .
    ?S ?P ?X .
    ?S ?P ?Y
} => {
    ?X owl:sameAs ?Y
} .

# Fuse: two values stated to be different would be a contradiction.
{
    ?P a owl:FunctionalProperty .
    ?S ?P ?X .
    ?S ?P ?Y .
    ?X owl:differentFrom ?Y
} => false .
